## July Showstopper: Dried Fish and Botulinum Risks

This month brought a worrying recall of dried herring fish, flagged for improper evisceration which could lead to Clostridium botulinum contamination. Prime Food Processing LLC issued a Class I recall for this very reason. Botulinum toxin is a significant health risk, capable of causing severe illness or death.

## Salmonella Strikes Again

In yet another setback, Utz Quality Foods, LLC faced multiple recalls for their Dirty Brand Salt and Vinegar Potato Chips and Zapp's Bayou Blackened Ranch Kettle Chips. Subsequent tests revealed potential Salmonella contamination. This particular pathogen has already accounted for 196 recalls this year, solidifying its position as a persistent challenge in food safety.

## Repeat Offenders Spotlight

Spring & Mulberry Inc., crowned one of this year's worst offenders, made headlines again with recalls of their Mango Chili and Mulberry Fennel date-sweetened chocolate products. Both were flagged for possible Salmonella contamination, adding to the company's troubling track record of 11 Class I recalls.

## Allergen Awareness: The Hidden Danger

Allergens remain another critical concern. George J Howe Company, Inc. issued a recall for their No Salt Sunflower Seeds due to the presence of undeclared cashew allergens. Undeclared allergens have resulted in 297 recalls this year, emphasizing the ongoing need for stringent labeling and manufacturing practices.

## Who's to Blame?

While the FDA continues its regulatory oversight, data indicates that certain companies are repeatedly falling short. With 93 repeat offenders and leading brands like Spring & Mulberry Inc. earning failing grades for safety, the industry's commitment to quality assurance must be questioned.

## Conclusion and Call to Action

Food safety is a shared responsibility among manufacturers, regulators, and consumers. Vigilance, robust manufacturing practices, and timely recalls are vital. Keep an eye out for updates from Shelf Check as we continue to monitor this critical issue.
